well it happened  the snyder cut is real the internet pipe  dream conspiracy theory  internet fan campaign that would not say  quit zack snyder's justice league  director's cut will be shot  produced and released on hbo max finally  giving fans what they've been clamoring  for  the snyder cut back in 2017 when zack  snyder had to step away from his  directing duties on justice league to  deal with a family tragedy  warner brothers tapped marvel director  joss whedon to finish the shoot  and in doing so completely reshaped the  storyline adding more comedy  and leaving many fans cold with this  drastically new approach  well i know you didn't bring me back  because you like me  i don't ever since the film's lackluster  critical reception  and tepid box office result the fans  have been  railing on the internet to release the  snyder cut going to extreme  lengths to hopefully entice studio execs  to release  or finish the film as snyder had  intended  but while many will rejoice the snyder  cut there are countless examples of fan  service gone wrong or worse placating  the most toxic voices on the internet  and severely damaging creators  brands and the films in the process so  with so much good and bad coming from  fandom influence  it begs the question when did fandom  become such a force in entertainment and  what does that mean for the future of  movie making  first let's break down what we mean when  we say fan service  this is essentially something in a work  of fiction or in a fictional series  which is intentionally added to please  the audience  of that ip this might be as simple as  things like  a beloved video game musical q in the  animated castlevania netflix series  or the captain america winter soldier on  your left call back from the finale of  avengers end game on your left  or the iconic we are the walking  deadline  from amc's the walking dead in the  comics  when lead character rick grimes utters  the line  it re-contextualized the entire series  by stating the walking dead  were in fact the living we are the  walking dead  so when rick said the same famous line  in the show  it was a moment that was anticipated and  rejoiced by the walking dead's most  die-hard fans whether it's a simple line  or an easter egg prop in the background  of a shot  each moment serves the story but more  importantly it gives the fans a little  love tap to say  hey we see you and this is for you  and although these slight nods are  prevalent in modern day franchise  blockbusters  fanservice has been a part of modern  entertainment from the moment people  started recreating books  video games graphic novels and theme  park rides for movies and television  even going back to before the golden age  of hollywood  consider this fun easter egg and gone  with the wind arguably one of the very  first blockbusters  in the film scarlett's first husband  charles hamilton's death certificate  is signed by wade hampton in the book  charles and scarlett have a son wade  hampton hamilton  director victor fleming added this as  just a little nod to the fans of the  book who would have also have noticed  that all of scarlett's children save her  daughter with rhett were written out  fans love it and rave when they like  what's done  adding depth and authenticity to this  property they already adore  but speaking of gone with the wind it  actually brings us to the darker side of  fanservice and where we take a look at  what happens when things go wrong  either in fan perception or in reality  one of the more obvious ways that  studios try to please fans is when  casting characters they loved on the  page  and try as they might studios often  disappoint  but before we talk about how that  happened with gone with the wind  let's talk about one of the earliest  casting controversies in hollywood  history  in 1921 rudolph valentino became the  world's first bonafide silent film  sex symbol with his portrayal of the  sheik  but even back then when his casting was  announced fans of the novel it was based  upon were up in arms they were convinced  that the italian hunk would not pull off  the menacing and seductive chic  they'd been swooning over on the pages  of edith maud hull's novel  and yes much of the nation was  absolutely  livid when vivian lee a brit mind you  was announced to play the american  plantation princess scarlett o'hara and  gone with the wind  in 1939 gone with the wind was an  international bestseller and just about  every  15 to 30 something white american woman  saw herself as the fiery young debutant  great balls of fire don't bother me  anymore don't call me sugar and it was  these same women who were simply  incensed that after an extensive casting  process  which included the likes of lucille ball  joan crawford  betty davis catherine hepburn and lana  turner  lee a crumpet eating tomato  not tomato on the wrong side of the  boston tea party brit  was chosen fans railed against the  choice there were letter writing  campaigns and threats to boycott the  film  but in the end gone with the wind would  go on to become  the greatest commercial and critical hit  of its day  frankly my hair i don't give a damn lee  would be heralded for her performance  and go on to win  one of the eight oscars the film was  awarded but  that was after the premiere the  controversy is mostly forgotten these  days  given all the current controversy around  the film not surprisingly  but that dust up was what director  sharon mcguire used to clap back at  anyone who questioned her choice to cast  a skinny texan  renee zellwigger as the uber british  bridget jones for bridget jones's diary  but just like scarlett our little  bridget was also  perfectly cast today we think of heath  ledger's role in the dark knight as a  reason not to rail against superhero  casting announcements  but lee valentino and ledger show  that sometimes it pays to wait and see  the end result  before rushing to judgment before  actually seeing  a performance of one of their beloved  stories  but you can't really blame fans for  being frustrated  when what they get on the big screen  isn't exactly  what they envisioned in their heads  we've become so accustomed to ordering  up our latest desire  anytime we please when we're told that's  just not how it works  we find it hard to swallow this is why  we struggle  accepting the fact that we can't order  up a live-action hercules remake like a  popeye's chicken sandwich  from postmates but in all seriousness  storytelling is about vision  characters and craft and that isn't  always served  by trying to know what a fan will think  about not giving wolverine yellow tights  or halle berry having a south african  accent  and as these swings and fan sentiment  can severely damage  or boost the earning potential of a  franchise  mistakes have been made and there have  been casualties on both sides  cleaning up the timelines according to  many one of the most egregious examples  of fan indulgence  is the final trilogy of the skywalker  saga  the series kicked off by pleasing fans  and critics alike with the force awakens  and then well then came the polarizing  the last jedi  followed by the movie that tried to give  fans exactly what they thought they  wanted but for many fell short  though the screenwriters of the rise of  skywalker and director jj abrams would  go on to say that they were not trying  to course correct  many of the major plot points either  conflicted with  or retconned the bits of the last jedi  that fans had to cry after the last jedi  fans were livid change.org petitions  were created  boycotts were threatened and some very  ambitious people put forth a campaign  to remake the entire film which begs  another question  why do people care so much about the  story of space samurais  in 2007 patton oswalt penned a very  funny  cutting but surprisingly prescient  article about geek culture  and fandom called wake up geek culture  it's time to die  in it he litigates the idea that geekdom  is no longer underground  the internet has made anyone who wants  to be an expert on something  actually an expert with just the click  of a mouse and ownership is  even more easily achieved i'm a star  wars fan  i'm a trekkie i'm an anime head and in a  few short clicks  you can be because of this more people  have been seduced by the idea that they  own this content as a part of their  identity  sorry to be the bearer of bad news but  disney owns star wars and there's  nothing  we or even george lucas can do about it  we don't own it  but marketing toy t-shirt and  memorabilia companies have  no problem whatsoever feeding our  addiction  to feel like we do i don't know i  thought they look kind of  dashy that's why these same fans and hey  we count ourselves among them  get supremely upset if they feel like  what they love  or more importantly who they are is not  being cared for  in a way that they would hope because  it's a part of them  experts walk away just walk away  oswald said anyone can become an expert  about anything  instantly in the 1980s you couldn't get  up to speed on an entire  genre in a weekend you had to wait month  to month for issues of the watchmen to  come out  we don't agree with oswald's ultimate  proposal that geekdom should get suckier  to combat this  but he made a point that resonates about  the democratization of fandom  and what it is done including the real  harm it is inflicted on some innocent  people  who are just trying to entertain star  wars actor john boyega came out recently  to plainly say  it was an abdication of duty for studios  not to combat  the online harassment women and people  of color face  when fandoms attacked them online in the  end  the creator fandom echo chamber is never  going to stop  but as much as creatives should keep  fans in the front of their mind  they also need the freedom to take risks  to subvert  expectations to cast an australian actor  mostly known for brokeback mountain  as dc's most feared villain to put a  whole new version of spider-man  on screen in an animated style the world  had never seen  to take wolverine and paint him as an  aging down on his luck  nihilist former hero and maybe they  could even kill snoke  with great power comes great  responsibility  we can rejoice in the snyder cut and in  the fact that sonic will come out  without creepy muscles and creepy teeth  but  we can also acknowledge that giving fans  what they  want can be a fraught path and not  always one that leads to the most  incredible movies no matter where you  land on the debate  we can all be superheroes by helping out  to make sure fandom  is welcoming to everyone we can do it  and we must try to do it quickly  do or do not  there is no try okay okay master yoda we  will do  it's the least we can do as your baby is  the best bit of fan service we've had in  a long time  yes we know we know he's not an actual  baby  you

